WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.624
Welcome to Tech Talk. And today's topic is about&nbsp;phishing and ransomware. We want to answer the&nbsp;question:

00:00:06.624 --> 00:00:12.240
Should you pay a ransom? And so you've&nbsp;
talked about this in a prior video about how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:16.140
defend yourself against it. But now we want&nbsp;
to say, let's assume it's already happened.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:19.205
That's bad news. Where do we go with that?&nbsp;

00:00:19.205 --> 00:00:21.885
Yeah, this is the problem, because every one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:26.757
of us could potentially be a target. So we need&nbsp;
to understand what we're going to do, if in fact&nbsp;it occurs. 

00:00:26.757 --> 00:00:30.060
The funny thing is-- and maybe not&nbsp;
the funny thing that happened is -- is I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:36.060
a friend who was hit by a ransomware attack. And&nbsp;
they demanded money, just like you always hear.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:43.020
And they had encrypted his database-- or&nbsp;
his laptop. In the end, he decided not to&nbsp;pay. 

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:49.620
And I want to really understand better from a&nbsp;security perspective, what were his options?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:53.900
What were the things that he could or should have done&nbsp;to make that decision easier? 

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:59.760
Yeah, absolutely.&nbsp;Well, first of all, so I feel bad for your friend,&nbsp;but your friend is not alone. A lot of people get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:05.580
hit by this. And we've got cases where it's an&nbsp;
individual. So it's an individual person who gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:10.860
hit. And then we've got cases where organizations&nbsp;
get hit, and it could be everything in between.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:17.820
We've got attackers who are very unsophisticated&nbsp;and they send out an attack to just snag anybody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:20.607
that they possibly can. And that's probably what&nbsp;
happened to your friend. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.607 --> 00:01:24.064
So they're a small fish they're going after, but there's a lot of them.&nbsp;

00:01:24.064 --> 00:01:33.400
Exactly. They make it up in volume. Or you've got&nbsp;the others that may be trying for very specific&nbsp;targets, high-value targets. And they may be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:38.700
very elite hackers that are doing this. And they're&nbsp;
breaking into systems using zero-day attacks and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:43.640
things like that in order to get in to go after&nbsp;
what they know are the keys to the kingdom. 

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:47.820
Well, there have been some cases in the news that are&nbsp;really gotten a lot of play. I think you mentioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:49.922
one of those before we started recording. 

00:01:49.922 --> 00:01:53.624
Yeah,&nbsp;exactly. There was one with the Colonial Pipeline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:59.520
organization which shut down gas production and&nbsp;distribution-- I guess probably just distribution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:07.726
for much of the Southeast for a number of weeks.&nbsp;And that was as a result of an attack related to&nbsp;ransomware.

00:02:07.726 --> 00:02:12.180
But they were using --were they&nbsp;
attacking the control systems or some other?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:13.559
What was their strategy for that? 

00:02:13.559 --> 00:02:23.304
Based upon the&nbsp;information we have from public sources, it was a&nbsp;&nbsp;case where they attacked the billing system.&nbsp;So actually, the oil could still be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.304 --> 00:02:27.660
distributed, technically, but if they couldn't charge for it,&nbsp;they had no records for it. And nobody wants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:32.400
to run a business like that. So they had to wait&nbsp;
until they could get that system recovered, until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.023
they could go ahead and start operating again.

00:02:34.023 --> 00:02:35.889
So&nbsp;they paid. [Jeff] They paid. 

00:02:35.889 --> 00:02:38.432
They paid, and in the end what happened?

00:02:38.432 --> 00:02:43.260
Well, so there's really three&nbsp;things that could happen if you pay. One is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:49.080
what sounds like the good case, is you get your data&nbsp;
back. That's what everybody's hoping for. I pay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:55.260
I get my data, end of story. There's another case&nbsp;
that can happen here where you get a decryption&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.260 --> 00:03:00.000
tool of some sort. That's what happened in the&nbsp;
Colonial Pipeline case, again, based upon the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.640
publicly available information. And the decryption&nbsp;
tool turned out to be so slow and so ineffective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:11.940
that they had to rely on their own backups anyway.&nbsp;
So they paid, but they really didn't get much for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:19.200
what they paid. And then the third option is&nbsp;
you get absolutely nothing. The ransomware&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:24.240
attacker takes your money and gives you nothing&nbsp;
in return. So now not only have you lost your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:29.280
precious memories, your photos, or your corporate&nbsp;
data, you also lost your money in the process.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:36.240
And you would think this looks like one possible&nbsp;
good outcome because I got what I was after. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:41.820
I'll tell you, what this also does is it signals&nbsp;
to the bad guys "This is a potential soft target".&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:46.860
Because your system was vulnerable this time,&nbsp;
maybe you haven't fixed it for the next time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:53.460
and we already know you're willing to pay. So&nbsp;
if I get you between the crosshairs again, maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:57.467
you'll pay again. So you've now signaled to the&nbsp;
world you're a sucker. 

00:03:57.467 --> 00:04:04.920
In the case of my buddy,&nbsp;he decided not to pay. And that's because he&nbsp;followed some of your advice. Maybe that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:10.920
say how to create really good backups. And so&nbsp;
he did have some data lost, but not completely.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:18.268
What do you think about his approach and what&nbsp;
would you advise to our viewers to avoid becoming&nbsp;his sort of victim? 

00:04:18.268 --> 00:04:22.680
The best thing you can do is&nbsp;make sure you've got good backups-- and 

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:27.900
you have&nbsp;&nbsp;to start that now. You can't do that after you've&nbsp;been attacked. It's too late to do a backup then.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:33.780
So that would be the first thing you could do. The&nbsp;
next thing you could do is try to make sure that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:39.780
the information you have is well protected with&nbsp;
multi-factor authentication and things like that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:45.240
that we talked about in a previous talk. Those are&nbsp;
the kinds of things that will help protect you in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:51.300
advance. But once you've already been attacked,&nbsp;
if they evaded those defenses, or you didn't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:54.687
those defenses in place, then you're back to the&nbsp;
question of "pay or no pay." 

00:04:54.687 --> 00:04:58.491
There's also a legal&nbsp;and moral question to be considered, too, right?&nbsp;

00:04:58.491 --> 00:05:08.040
There definitely is. In fact, if you think about&nbsp;it this way: If I pay this guy, I've basically&nbsp;rewarded him for bad behavior and there's no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:12.240
incentive for him to stop doing it. He's going to&nbsp;
keep doing it and keep doing it and keep doing it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:16.860
as long as he gets paid. Now, I understand as&nbsp;
an individual, though, you're not concerned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:21.420
necessarily about what happens to everybody&nbsp;
else, you just want your data back. If you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:27.180
an organization, you need your company running&nbsp;
again. But there is that consequence. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:32.580
there's also the legal part. And I'm not a lawyer,&nbsp;
so you should consult your own lawyer on this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:37.680
but there's some discussion about whether it's&nbsp;
even legal to pay a ransom. And why might it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:43.680
be illegal? Well, because the money you're giving&nbsp;
could be going-- well, is going to criminals, for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:48.720
sure --it could be funding a criminal enterprise.&nbsp;
It could be funding terrorists, for that matter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:55.320
So we don't know this kind of thing when you pay,&nbsp;
but you could be giving money to people that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:59.013
literally enemies of the state and therefore you&nbsp;
would be complicit. 

00:05:59.013 --> 00:06:06.780
Wow. One thing before we wrap,&nbsp;I wanted to bring up is that in the case of my&nbsp;buddy, he was looking in an encrypted hard drive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:12.480
But there are other cases where ransomware&nbsp;
comes into play. For example, someone hijacks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:18.660
your email and is demanding payment in order to&nbsp;
retrieve access. Or say, for example, they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:24.540
an image repository and they've done that. What&nbsp;
is your advice to avoid becoming susceptible to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:25.198
that kind of attack? 

00:06:25.198 --> 00:06:32.737
Again, it's going to be&nbsp;backups. Because in those cases, if I've got a&nbsp;repository and someone hijacks my credentials to&nbsp;that 

00:06:32.737 --> 00:06:40.848
repository and says, "I'm not going to give&nbsp;you access to your own credentials because I've&nbsp;changed the password now," then the only thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.848 --> 00:06:45.600
you could really do to avoid having to pay and end&nbsp;up with all the problems that we talked about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:51.840
is make sure you have a way to recover that.&nbsp;
If you have stored those in another service,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:56.820
like maybe another cloud repository, and you've&nbsp;
not used the same password for both of them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.820 --> 00:07:00.780
then there's a good chance that maybe you&nbsp;
could recover from another source. But the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:06.240
thing you can't rely on necessarily is being able&nbsp;
to go to the provider. If it was a free account,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:11.940
they're obviously not going to help you, because&nbsp;
as the old saying goes, "If you're not paying for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:18.360
it, you're the product, not the customer."&nbsp;
And if you didn't pay for that photo site,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:24.300
that's storing all your photos, then you are not&nbsp;
the customer. And products don't get to call up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:27.079
and complain. So that's really your information.&nbsp;

00:07:27.079 --> 00:07:35.040
That's really true. And one thing you can do--&nbsp;to keep in mind --is that a lot of even the free services have a download capability where you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:40.740
download that. You can also use, for example, if&nbsp;
you're using email, you can have it replicated to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:46.680
your laptop and then which you can backup. These&nbsp;are ways that you can recover it locally. 

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:52.080
I think that pretty much covers our strategy. Oh,&nbsp;
one other thing: Multi-factor authentication. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:55.920
mentioned that--could you kind of explain really&nbsp;
quickly? For those who haven't seen the video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:57.332
you can watch it here. 

00:07:57.332 --> 00:08:03.398
Yeah. Multi-factor&nbsp;authentication says I'm going to use something&nbsp;&nbsp;more than just your password alone because someone&nbsp;might steal 

00:08:03.398 --> 00:08:10.320
your password or guess it and then&nbsp;change it and then not tell you. But if I've used a second factor, or even a third factor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:14.400
such as something I have like a phone that's&nbsp;
been pre-registered and I'm going to send&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:19.020
a text message, or a push notification to that&nbsp;
phone. If you don't have that in your possession,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:24.660
you can't log in, or a biometric like your&nbsp;
face or your finger. And the phone uses that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:29.340
to unlock the account. If you're using these&nbsp;
other kinds of things, it's much harder for an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:35.820
attacker to duplicate those kind of authentication&nbsp;
capabilities. So you could guard against that in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:41.220
the first place. And then there's even yet other&nbsp;
types of ransomware attacks where I'm not stealing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:46.440
your data or your access, but I'm threatening&nbsp;
that I'm going to do a denial-of-service on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:52.140
your network. And in those cases, we need yet&nbsp;
different types of defenses to make sure that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:58.200
that we can block all the extraneous traffic&nbsp;
that's coming in and do it at as close to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:03.360
source as possible. So there's a lot of different&nbsp;
mitigations that happen here, but the number one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:05.132
thing is to make sure you're prepared. 

00:09:05.132 --> 00:09:09.223
That's some&nbsp;excellent advice, and I think that's a wrap for&nbsp;&nbsp;us. Thanks again. 

00:09:09.223 --> 00:09:14.006
And if you have topics you'd&nbsp;like to hear on Tech Talk, be sure and drop us&nbsp;a comment below. 

00:09:14.006 --> 00:09:18.643
And before you leave, please do&nbsp;us a favor and remember to hit Like and Subscribe.

